00:36:22.113 --> 00:36:23.681 align:start size:81% position:19%
that because of
the way it spreads

00:36:23.681 --> 00:36:25.383
that it would end up there?

00:36:25.383 --> 00:36:27.885 align:start size:84% position:16%
- No, I don't, I would
be lying if I said

00:36:27.885 --> 00:36:30.688
I had the foresight of where
my engagement was gonna go.

00:36:30.688 --> 00:36:32.957
What was clear from my training

00:36:32.957 --> 00:36:35.860 align:start size:97% position:3%
is I was studying psychology
of decision making.

00:36:35.860 --> 00:36:37.795 align:start size:94% position:6%
So one of the things
when you study psychology

00:36:37.795 --> 00:36:40.298
of decision making, one
of the things you do is

00:36:40.298 --> 00:36:42.400 align:start size:88% position:13%
you study what's
called rational choice.

00:36:42.400 --> 00:36:45.203
So for example if I
prefer car A to car B

00:36:45.203 --> 00:36:47.338
and I prefer car B to car C,

00:36:47.338 --> 00:36:49.807 align:start size:84% position:16%
it has to be that I
prefer car A to car C.

00:36:49.807 --> 00:36:51.576
That's called the
transitivity axiom.

00:36:51.576 --> 00:36:54.111 align:start size:84% position:16%
If I violate this
order of preference,

00:36:54.111 --> 00:36:56.480 align:start size:81% position:19%
then I am being
quote, irrational.

00:36:56.480 --> 00:36:59.350
So part of my training
in my doctoral studies

00:36:59.350 --> 00:37:01.886 align:start size:88% position:13%
was to study psychology
of decision making

00:37:01.886 --> 00:37:03.087
or behavioral decision theory

00:37:03.087 --> 00:37:05.456 align:start size:84% position:16%
which by the way
led to a Nobel Prize

00:37:05.456 --> 00:37:07.692 align:start size:84% position:16%
to one of my
professors at Cornell,

00:37:07.692 --> 00:37:11.562 align:start size:97% position:3%
Richard Thaler and so I
come from a group of folks,

00:37:11.562 --> 00:37:13.497 align:start size:88% position:13%
several of whom who
have won the Nobel Prize

00:37:13.497 --> 00:37:15.700
for demonstrating that
economic decision making

00:37:15.700 --> 00:37:17.001
is sometimes faulty.

00:37:17.001 --> 00:37:19.370
So it was already
part of my training

00:37:19.370 --> 00:37:22.039
to study irrational behavior.

00:37:22.039 --> 00:37:26.077
But what was a surprise to
me was that the irrationality

00:37:26.077 --> 00:37:29.947
would seep its way from
this very circumscript,

00:37:29.947 --> 00:37:34.952
you know mathematical axiom to
politics, to popular culture.

00:37:36.354 --> 00:37:39.390
So in a sense, I was trained
to look at human irrationality.

00:37:39.390 --> 00:37:40.858
I just didn't know
that I would go into

00:37:40.858 --> 00:37:42.727
all of these different fields.

00:37:42.727 --> 00:37:45.896 align:start position:0%
- Yeah have you been, we've
